set of $R.$ Every natural number is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105034/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105034"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37267/selection/48ed73fa8fff01bdc324b4f21abd0bd25d38fe050f7a804787ee07e14c349dd0" :answer/id #uuid "6bd53d33-1c8e-534e-9ce6-4529042d038f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37267/selection/4c09ef323dd53803d71b0c6f839afd3fa7c67c7a3c52acd9fb673fd4aafc2a26" :answer/id #uuid "913cbce1-d5c3-528b-a007-ec79a69a2193" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37267/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer/id #uuid "cefdc396-cdb8-534a-94b9-3b014581773a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37267/selection/68ee7990c679da4221e974bf737d4c65351e6315adbede6c3f8bf610a59bde12" :answer/id #uuid "28c111ac-4ff1-53f3-b75f-8dbc64b928af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37267/selection/6578e6ba0baf24b357651e14e1811fc4e1af585d8babc3d67097617d0e105e7a" :answer/id #uuid "63d1dcb1-9405-5992-ac49-781495952da9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37267/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2cd607a-2d8a-59d9-925b-b70565bf262c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37267/selection/4c09ef323dd53803d71b0c6f839afd3fa7c67c7a3c52acd9fb673fd4aafc2a26" :answer-field/choices ["q-37267/selection/48ed73fa8fff01bdc324b4f21abd0bd25d38fe050f7a804787ee07e14c349dd0" "q-37267/selection/4c09ef323dd53803d71b0c6f839afd3fa7c67c7a3c52acd9fb673fd4aafc2a26" "q-37267/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" "q-37267/selection/68ee7990c679da4221e974bf737d4c65351e6315adbede6c3f8bf610a59bde12" "q-37267/selection/6578e6ba0baf24b357651e14e1811fc4e1af585d8babc3d67097617d0e105e7a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37267" :question/id #uuid "5f2ce395-36f1-5103-8a97-86f354e329cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-37267" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={5,10,15,20,25,30,35,…}$ and $B={5n∣n\\in N,3\\le n\\le 7}?$\n\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊆B$\n- $A⊈B$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $B.$ Substituting the values $n\\in {3,4,5,6,7}$ into the formula $5n,$ we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=3 & \\,⇒\\,5(3)=15 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,5(4)=20 \\\\ n=5 & \\,⇒\\,5(5)=25 \\\\ n=6 & \\,⇒\\,5(6)=30 \\\\ n=7 & \\,⇒\\,5(7)=35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nB={15,20,25,30,35}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37267/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37267"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80845/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "f1fd0f94-107a-5513-91c7-7553805f2142" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80845/selection/4c09ef323dd53803d71b0c6f839afd3fa7c67c7a3c52acd9fb673fd4aafc2a26" :answer/id #uuid "ece62413-ee86-5be4-bebf-0e7588e28923" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80845/selection/68ee7990c679da4221e974bf737d4c65351e6315adbede6c3f8bf610a59bde12" :answer/id #uuid "8284ced4-6a6f-5a6f-8ede-4dff48224ea7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80845/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "07cefd1a-a90f-5344-b0af-d3883e1ae213" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80845/selection/48ed73fa8fff01bdc324b4f21abd0bd25d38fe050f7a804787ee07e14c349dd0" :answer/id #uuid "5d408e83-9890-59fd-937d-71c5c9bf1370" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80845/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa18f640-0d06-58ad-a0c0-856ff8c6e42a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80845/selection/68ee7990c679da4221e974bf737d4c65351e6315adbede6c3f8bf610a59bde12" :answer-field/choices ["q-80845/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-80845/selection/4c09ef323dd53803d71b0c6f839afd3fa7c67c7a3c52acd9fb673fd4aafc2a26" "q-80845/selection/68ee7990c679da4221e974bf737d4c65351e6315adbede6c3f8bf610a59bde12" "q-80845/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-80845/selection/48ed73fa8fff01bdc324b4f21abd0bd25d38fe050f7a804787ee07e14c349dd0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80845" :question/id #uuid "9a90c52c-818b-5155-a8eb-42e1d51e8eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80845" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in Z∣n+1\\ge -1}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n-2\\ge -4}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $B⊇A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $n+1\\ge -1,$ that is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge -4,$ That is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nB={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80845/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80845"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37260/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "d350a45f-c83f-54ec-b6a5-57f2369bcbdb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37260/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "eb2a9b2b-512b-50ac-911c-5baa1df70aab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37260/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "5080db1c-caf7-54c0-882d-6633c1dcbebb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37260/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "a6051aed-845e-52ec-9b25-e9a8ee913aad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37260/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "50492bf0-6806-5ae6-a0bb-9740e4aa95dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37260/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d3be556-e704-5b99-909e-015533bd7c65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37260/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-37260/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-37260/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-37260/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-37260/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-37260/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37260" :question/id #uuid "dccd9da3-50bd-5ed9-bb93-47775cde65de" :question/math-academy-id "q-37260" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N:3\\le x<9}$ and $B={x\\in N:3x<10}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $∅⊆A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all natural numbers greater than or equal to $3$ but less than $9.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={3,4,5,6,7,8}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers which satisfy the inequality $3x<10.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $4\\in A$ but $4\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $1\\in B$ but $1\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆A$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37260/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37260"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105064/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "ddd387eb-ba17-5d21-b2de-737f5a252db5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105064/selection/6578e6ba0baf24b357651e14e1811fc4e1af585d8babc3d67097617d0e105e7a" :answer/id #uuid "02a66fd6-63a7-5f02-aac2-051a0cc4be24" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105064/selection/68ee7990c679da4221e974bf737d4c65351e6315adbede6c3f8bf610a59bde12" :answer/id #uuid "52d1851e-405b-56ae-b75d-ff639bb4de5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105064/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2806b1b0-bb56-5070-b3ac-229501b50366" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105064/selection/4c09ef323dd53803d71b0c6f839afd3fa7c67c7a3c52acd9fb673fd4aafc2a26" :answer/id #uuid "2845a4bf-3b5e-5cf9-a985-fc686a662a91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105064/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "62b3f659-2e8b-5bf8-849c-7bf158e0d774" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105064/selection/4c09ef323dd53803d71b0c6f839afd3fa7c67c7a3c52acd9fb673fd4aafc2a26" :answer-field/choices ["q-105064/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-105064/selection/6578e6ba0baf24b357651e14e1811fc4e1af585d8babc3d67097617d0e105e7a" "q-105064/selection/68ee7990c679da4221e974bf737d4c65351e6315adbede6c3f8bf610a59bde12" "q-105064/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-105064/selection/4c09ef323dd53803d71b0c6f839afd3fa7c67c7a3c52acd9fb673fd4aafc2a26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105064" :question/id #uuid "90026120-6d23-527b-8cd4-e6e4979a06e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105064" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in N∣n-2\\ge 4}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n+2\\ge 5}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $∅⊆A$\n- $B⊇A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge 4,$ that is to say, $n\\ge 6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={6,7,8,9,10,11,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\ge 5,$ That is to say, $n\\ge 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={3,4,5,6,7,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $3\\in B$ but $3\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆A$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105064/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105064"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-1210"] :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\n- $∅⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13195"] :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $S=\\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}: {-3} \\leq 2n+1 \\leq 7 \\}?$\n\n- $\\{ -2, 0, 4 \\}$\n\n- $\\{ 0, 3 \\}$\n\n- $\\{ -3, 1, 3 \\}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $S.$ Solving the given inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\le 2n+1 & \\le 7 \\\\ -4\\le 2n & \\le 6 \\\\ -2\\le n & \\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $S$ contains all integers between $-2$ and $3$ (including $-2$ and $3$):\n\n$$\nS = \\{ -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}\n$$\n\nA set is a subset of $S$ if all its elements are elements of $S.$ With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $\\{ -2,0,4 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $4 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\{ 0,3 \\}$ is a subset of $S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $\\{ -3,1,3 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $-3 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13196"] :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $A = \\{ a, b, c \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/8323a4292a4f06d66d456bfce1906cbc7e9933a3fac41dd5fb643e13d25f6a6e.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13197"] :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13198"] :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{ n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{ n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}?$\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$\n\n- $B\\nsubseteq A$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10,$ that is, $n\\geq 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5,$ that is, $n\\geq 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A.$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
